// The Bit allocator (runtime/alloc/alloc.bit, #1362) — the Stage-2 port of
// runtime/alloc.zig, exercised as a real linked binary.
//
// The module under test is imported from `../../../runtime/alloc`, so this
// compiles the SAME source the runtime will ship rather than a copy.
//
// WHY THIS TEST CAN RUN ON EVERY TARGET. `runtime/alloc/alloc.bit` is deliberately
// platform-free: the page seam is inverted so the caller supplies mapped pages
// (see the module header — `syscall` is Linux-only and `extern function` is
// Darwin-only, and both reject even an uncalled declaration, so no single
// source form can carry both). That inversion is what lets one test exercise
// the real allocator on aarch64-macos, aarch64-linux and x86_64-linux without a
// line of per-target code: this file plays the role of the OS, handing out
// page-aligned chunks from a bump arena instead of from mmap. Every byte of
// allocator POLICY — classification, alignment, the free-list shape, the carve
// order, the counters — is the shipped code.
//
// The real page providers (`runtime/alloc/linux`, `runtime/alloc/darwin`) are
// exercised separately, on real kernel memory, by `../alloclinux` and
// `../allocdarwin` — they cannot be reached from here because a `syscall` and
// an `extern function` cannot coexist in one module.
//
// Nine properties, all deterministic:
//
//   (a) SIZE-CLASS TABLE — the 37 classes are dumped as a position-weighted
//       checksum, so a single wrong or transposed entry changes the output.
//       `runtime/alloc.bit` computes the table arithmetically where the Zig
//       spells it as a literal array (Bit cannot index a table under
//       `@nosplit`), which makes this the one place a typo could hide.
//       `scripts/alloc-difftable.sh` additionally diffs all 37 entries against
//       the Zig itself.
//
//   (b) TABLE INVARIANTS — the classes strictly increase, and every power of
//       two from 8 to 16384 IS a class. That second one is the invariant the
//       Zig's comment rests its alignment guarantee on ("any supported
//       alignment is itself a class, so a satisfying slot always exists"), so
//       it is checked rather than assumed.
//
//   (c) CLASSIFY CONTRACT — over a matrix of sizes x alignments, the class
//       `allocClassify` picks must satisfy `cs >= alignUp(size, alignment)` and
//       `cs % alignment == 0`, and must be the SMALLEST class that does. That
//       is the full specification of the function, checked without a golden:
//       a classify that returned a merely-adequate class rather than the best
//       one still passes the first two conditions and fails this.
//
//   (d) ALIGNMENT — every address handed out is aligned to the requested
//       alignment, across every power of two from 8 to 4096 and sizes that
//       straddle class boundaries. This is the property the GC depends on.
//
//   (e) REUSE — freeing a slot and allocating the same class again returns the
//       IDENTICAL address (the Zig's own unit test asserts exactly this), which
//       proves the intrusive free list recycles rather than re-carving.
//
//   (f) CARVE — one span of a given class yields exactly `spanBytes / classSize`
//       distinct slots, all inside the span and all class-size apart. This is
//       the metadata shape the collector's sweep walks back onto the list.
//
//   (g) COUNTERS — `liveBytes` tracks outstanding bytes exactly and returns to
//       0 once everything is freed; `mappedBytes` equals the spans actually
//       taken. The Zig's leak metric.
//
//   (i) DIRECT-MAPPED (LARGE) PATH — requests above allocMaxSmall bypass the
//       size classes: mapped straight from the OS, rounded up to a page, and
//       accounted separately. Nothing else in this file reaches that path, and
//       the differential covers only the `allocLargeLen` arithmetic and not its
//       composition with the counters, so the Zig's own "large direct-mapped
//       allocations are page aligned and unmapped on free" unit test is
//       mirrored here: page alignment, exact live/mapped accounting in BOTH
//       directions, and the full rounded length being writable memory.
//
//   (j) PER-SPAN OCCUPANCY — the property #2374 exists to establish. Every slot
//       of one size class is taken across THREE spans, then freed in a
//       scrambled order, and after every single free each span's own free count
//       must equal a tally this file computes from the slot addresses alone.
//       The independence is the point: `allocSpanOf` is a mask over the
//       address, so a take or give that credited the wrong span leaves every
//       aggregate over the class exactly right — `liveBytes`, the class total,
//       even `sum over spans of (total - free)` — and only a per-span
//       comparison sees it. Mutation-tested: masking one bit too few in
//       `heapGiveSmallLocked` fails this and nothing else in the corpus.
//       The scrambled order is a Fisher-Yates shuffle rather than a stride, so
//       it stays a permutation if the slots-per-span count ever moves; a stride
//       that stopped being coprime would free an address twice.
//
//   (h) CHURN WITH SENTINELS — a ring of live allocations is recycled 20000
//       times; each allocation carries a unique tag in its first word which
//       must still be there at free time. If the allocator ever handed the same
//       slot out twice, one tag would overwrite the other and the count of
//       intact sentinels would drop. This is the Zig's stress test ported, and
//       the first word is exactly where the intrusive next-pointer lives, so it
//       is also the byte the free list is most likely to corrupt.
//
// Under BIT_GC=stress the collector fires at every safepoint. The whole arena
// is a live `[]i64` holding raw addresses; it must stay rooted across every
// collection while only untraced `int` addresses reach the allocator, so a
// rooting or pointer-map bug shows up as a wrong answer rather than a crash.
//
// SINGLE-THREADED BY DESIGN. Mutual exclusion is the lock's property, not the
// allocator's, and `tests/stress/spinlock` already proves it under contention.
// Holding the lock across this file's non-`@nosplit` arena helper could reach a
// safepoint, and a `@nosplit` spinner cannot yield to release it (the deadlock
// hazard documented in runtime/spinlock.bit), so this test takes the lock on a
// single thread — which still exercises the real acquire/release path around
// every allocation.

import {
  allocNumClasses, allocMaxSmall, allocSpanBytes, heapWords,
  allocClassSize, allocAlignUp, allocNonZero, allocClassify, allocLargeLen,
  allocSpanHdrWords, allocSpanOf, allocSpanSlots,
  allocSpanClass, allocSpanTotal, allocSpanFree,
  heapInit, heapLiveBytes, heapMappedBytes,
  heapTakeSmallLocked, heapGiveSmallLocked, heapRefillLocked,
  heapLargeAllocLocked, heapLargeFreeLocked,
  heapCheckSpansLocked, heapSpanViolations,
} from "../../../runtime/alloc"
import { spinAcquire, spinRelease, spinUnlocked } from "../../../runtime"

const PAGE = 4096
// One span is consumed per size class on that class's first allocation (the
// Zig behaves identically), and property (e) touches all 37 classes, so the
// arena must cover every class plus the carve span, property (j)'s three and
// the large path.
const MAX_SPANS = 48
const SPAN_WORDS = 16384          // allocSpanBytes / 8
// MAX_SPANS * SPAN_WORDS + 2048 alignment slack + 262144 words (2MB) for the
// direct-mapped requests in property (i), which bypass spans entirely.
const ARENA_WORDS = 1050624
const RING = 128
const CHURN = 20000

// Property (j). Class 28 is 4096-byte slots, so a span yields 31 of them once
// the header is carved out: three spans is 93 slots — enough for a scrambled
// order to interleave the spans repeatedly, small enough to re-check every
// span's count after every single free.
const OCC_CLASS = 28
const OCC_SPANS = 3

// Arena state words.
const A_NEXT = 0
const A_LIMIT = 1
const A_SPANS = 2

// The `*i64` addressing the 8-aligned word at `addr`. The test's own copy of
// the allocator's private helper — the allocator fabricates pointers the same
// way (§11.4: `*i64` arithmetic scales by 8).
function wordAt(addr: int): *i64 {
  let z: *i64 = nil
  return z + (addr / 8)
}

// Hand out `len` bytes aligned to `align` from the bump arena. This is the test
// standing in for the OS: it is exactly the contract `mmap` fulfils for the Zig
// (page-aligned, never reused until the process exits). Returns 0 when
// exhausted, as `mmap` returning null does.
function arenaTake(a: []i64, len: int, align: int): int {
  let addr = allocAlignUp(a[A_NEXT], align)
  if (addr + len > a[A_LIMIT]) { return 0 }
  a[A_NEXT] = addr + len
  return addr
}

// Take one span, counting it. Bounded by MAX_SPANS.
function spanTake(a: []i64): int {
  if (a[A_SPANS] >= MAX_SPANS) { return 0 }
  let s = arenaTake(a, allocSpanBytes, allocSpanBytes)
  if (s == 0) { return 0 }
  a[A_SPANS] = a[A_SPANS] + 1
  return s
}

// The composed allocator entry point: the glue that in the shipped runtime is
// `runtime/alloc/{linux,darwin}`'s `mapPages` in place of `arenaTake`. The lock is held across the whole
// operation INCLUDING the page take, exactly as the Zig holds it across its
// refill mmap (`acquire(); defer release();` spans all of `Heap.alloc`).
function heapAlloc(h: *i64, lk: *i32, a: []i64, size: int, alignment: int): int {
  spinAcquire(lk)
  let addr = 0
  let idx = allocClassify(size, alignment)
  if (idx < 0) {
    let len = allocLargeLen(size, PAGE)
    let m = arenaTake(a, len, PAGE)
    if (m != 0) {
      heapLargeAllocLocked(h, size, len)
      addr = m
    }
  } else {
    addr = heapTakeSmallLocked(h, idx, size)
    if (addr == 0) {
      let span = spanTake(a)
      if (span != 0) {
        heapRefillLocked(h, idx, span)
        addr = heapTakeSmallLocked(h, idx, size)
      }
    }
  }
  spinRelease(lk)
  return addr
}

// The mirror of `heapAlloc`. `size`/`alignment` must match the original request
// — the Zig's free contract, unchanged.
function heapFree(h: *i64, lk: *i32, addr: int, size: int, alignment: int) {
  spinAcquire(lk)
  let idx = allocClassify(size, alignment)
  if (idx < 0) {
    heapLargeFreeLocked(h, size, allocLargeLen(size, PAGE))
  } else {
    heapGiveSmallLocked(h, addr, idx, size)
  }
  spinRelease(lk)
}

function main() {
  // ---- (a) size-class table: a position-weighted checksum -------------------
  // Weighting by (i+1) makes the checksum sensitive to ORDER, so two
  // transposed classes change it; a plain sum would not.
  let tsum = 0
  let twsum = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < allocNumClasses) {
    let cs = allocClassSize(i)
    tsum = tsum + cs
    twsum = twsum + cs * (i + 1)
    i = i + 1
  }

  // ---- (b) table invariants -------------------------------------------------
  // Strictly increasing, first is 8, last is allocMaxSmall.
  let mono = 1
  let j = 1
  while (j < allocNumClasses) {
    if (allocClassSize(j) <= allocClassSize(j - 1)) { mono = 0 }
    j = j + 1
  }
  if (allocClassSize(0) != 8) { mono = 0 }
  if (allocClassSize(allocNumClasses - 1) != allocMaxSmall) { mono = 0 }

  // Every power of two in [8, allocMaxSmall] is itself a class — the invariant
  // the alignment guarantee rests on.
  let powsOk = 1
  let p = 8
  while (p <= allocMaxSmall) {
    let found = 0
    let k = 0
    while (k < allocNumClasses) {
      if (allocClassSize(k) == p) { found = 1 }
      k = k + 1
    }
    if (found == 0) { powsOk = 0 }
    p = p * 2
  }

  // ---- (c) classify contract: smallest class that fits AND divides ----------
  let classifyOk = 1
  let alignIdx = 0
  while (alignIdx < 10) {
    let al = 8 << alignIdx                       // 8 .. 4096
    let sz = 1
    while (sz <= allocMaxSmall) {
      let idx = allocClassify(sz, al)
      let needed = allocAlignUp(sz, al)
      if (idx < 0) {
        // Only legitimate when no class can satisfy it at all.
        let any = 0
        let k2 = 0
        while (k2 < allocNumClasses) {
          let cs2 = allocClassSize(k2)
          if (cs2 >= needed && cs2 % al == 0) { any = 1 }
          k2 = k2 + 1
        }
        if (any == 1) { classifyOk = 0 }
      } else {
        let cs = allocClassSize(idx)
        if (cs < needed) { classifyOk = 0 }
        if (cs % al != 0) { classifyOk = 0 }
        // No smaller class may also satisfy it.
        let k3 = 0
        while (k3 < idx) {
          let cs3 = allocClassSize(k3)
          if (cs3 >= needed && cs3 % al == 0) { classifyOk = 0 }
          k3 = k3 + 1
        }
      }
      sz = sz * 3 + 1                            // straddles class boundaries
    }
    alignIdx = alignIdx + 1
  }

  // ---- heap + arena ---------------------------------------------------------
  let hbuf = []i64(heapWords)
  let h = ptrOf(hbuf)
  let lbuf = []i32(1)
  let lk = ptrOf(lbuf)
  atomicStore(lk, spinUnlocked)
  heapInit(h)

  let arena = []i64(ARENA_WORDS)
  let abase = int(ptrOf(arena))
  let a = []i64(3)
  a[A_NEXT] = allocAlignUp(abase, allocSpanBytes)
  a[A_LIMIT] = abase + ARENA_WORDS * 8
  a[A_SPANS] = 0

  let initOk = 0
  if (heapLiveBytes(h) == 0 && heapMappedBytes(h) == 0) { initOk = 1 }

  // ---- (d) alignment across powers of two -----------------------------------
  let alignOk = 1
  let ai = 0
  while (ai < 10) {
    let al = 8 << ai                             // 8 .. 4096
    let si = 0
    while (si < 6) {
      let sz = 1
      if (si == 1) { sz = 7 }
      if (si == 2) { sz = 8 }
      if (si == 3) { sz = 17 }
      if (si == 4) { sz = 100 }
      if (si == 5) { sz = 1000 }
      let addr = heapAlloc(h, lk, a, sz, al)
      if (addr == 0) {
        alignOk = 0                              // arena exhausted: never deref
      } else {
        if (addr % al != 0) { alignOk = 0 }
        heapFree(h, lk, addr, sz, al)
      }
      si = si + 1
    }
    ai = ai + 1
  }
  let liveAfterAlign = heapLiveBytes(h)

  // ---- (e) reuse: free then re-alloc returns the same address ---------------
  let reuseOk = 1
  let ri = 0
  while (ri < allocNumClasses) {
    let cs = allocClassSize(ri)
    let p1 = heapAlloc(h, lk, a, cs, 8)
    if (p1 == 0) {
      reuseOk = 0                                // arena exhausted
    } else {
      if (heapLiveBytes(h) != cs) { reuseOk = 0 }
      heapFree(h, lk, p1, cs, 8)
      if (heapLiveBytes(h) != 0) { reuseOk = 0 }
      let p2 = heapAlloc(h, lk, a, cs, 8)
      if (p2 != p1) { reuseOk = 0 }              // recycled, not re-carved
      heapFree(h, lk, p2, cs, 8)
    }
    ri = ri + 1
  }

  // ---- (f) carve: one span yields exactly spanBytes/classSize distinct slots -
  // A fresh heap and a fresh span, so the count is unambiguous.
  let h2buf = []i64(heapWords)
  let h2 = ptrOf(h2buf)
  let l2buf = []i32(1)
  let lk2 = ptrOf(l2buf)
  atomicStore(lk2, spinUnlocked)
  heapInit(h2)
  let carveOk = 1
  let carveClass = 20                            // classSize 1024 -> 128 slots
  let cs4 = allocClassSize(carveClass)
  let span = spanTake(a)
  if (span == 0) { carveOk = 0 }
  spinAcquire(lk2)
  heapRefillLocked(h2, carveClass, span)
  spinRelease(lk2)
  if (heapMappedBytes(h2) != allocSpanBytes) { carveOk = 0 }
  // Not `allocSpanBytes / cs4`: the span header at the base costs the class its
  // first `ceil(hdrBytes / cs4)` slots (#2374).
  let want = allocSpanSlots(carveClass)
  let got = 0
  let prev = 0
  let drained = 0
  // Statically bounded: a correct list yields exactly `want` slots then 0, so
  // `want + 1` pops always suffice. The bound is not decoration — an allocator
  // that fails to unlink the slot it hands out re-serves the same address
  // forever, and without it this loop spins instead of failing (found by
  // mutation M5).
  let guard = 0
  while (drained == 0 && guard <= want) {
    spinAcquire(lk2)
    let s = heapTakeSmallLocked(h2, carveClass, cs4)
    spinRelease(lk2)
    if (s == 0) {
      drained = 1
    } else {
      // Inside the span, and clear of the header at its base.
      if (s < span + allocSpanHdrWords * 8) { carveOk = 0 }
      if (s + cs4 > span + allocSpanBytes) { carveOk = 0 }
      if (got > 0 && prev - s != cs4) { carveOk = 0 }   // descending, class-size apart
      prev = s
      got = got + 1
    }
    guard = guard + 1
  }
  if (drained == 0) { carveOk = 0 }                     // list never terminated
  if (got != want) { carveOk = 0 }

  // ---- (j) per-span occupancy across three spans ---------------------------
  // A third private heap, so the class owns exactly the three spans below and
  // the tally has no other traffic to account for.
  let h3buf = []i64(heapWords)
  let h3 = ptrOf(h3buf)
  let l3buf = []i32(1)
  let lk3 = ptrOf(l3buf)
  atomicStore(lk3, spinUnlocked)
  heapInit(h3)

  let occOk = 1
  let occCs = allocClassSize(OCC_CLASS)
  let perSpan = allocSpanSlots(OCC_CLASS)
  let occN = OCC_SPANS * perSpan
  let occSpan = []i64(OCC_SPANS)
  let occFreed = []i64(OCC_SPANS)
  let occAddr = []i64(occN)
  let occOwner = []i64(occN)

  // Three spans up front. Each must report the class it was carved for, the
  // slot count the class expects, and no slot handed out yet.
  let oi = 0
  while (oi < OCC_SPANS) {                              // bounded: OCC_SPANS
    let sp = spanTake(a)
    occFreed[oi] = 0
    if (sp == 0) {
      occOk = 0                                         // arena exhausted
    } else {
      occSpan[oi] = sp
      spinAcquire(lk3)
      heapRefillLocked(h3, OCC_CLASS, sp)
      spinRelease(lk3)
      if (allocSpanOf(sp) != sp) { occOk = 0 }          // spans are self-aligned
      if (allocSpanClass(sp) != OCC_CLASS) { occOk = 0 }
      if (allocSpanTotal(sp) != perSpan) { occOk = 0 }
      if (allocSpanFree(sp) != perSpan) { occOk = 0 }
    }
    oi = oi + 1
  }

  // Take every slot the three spans hold. Each address must belong to one of
  // them, by the mask alone.
  let ti = 0
  while (ti < occN) {                                   // bounded: occN
    spinAcquire(lk3)
    let addr = heapTakeSmallLocked(h3, OCC_CLASS, occCs)
    spinRelease(lk3)
    occAddr[ti] = addr
    occOwner[ti] = 0
    if (addr == 0) {
      occOk = 0
    } else {
      let owner = -1
      let oj = 0
      while (oj < OCC_SPANS) {                          // bounded: OCC_SPANS
        if (allocSpanOf(addr) == occSpan[oj]) { owner = oj }
        oj = oj + 1
      }
      if (owner < 0) { occOk = 0 } else { occOwner[ti] = owner }
    }
    ti = ti + 1
  }

  // Fully occupied: every span reads zero free, and one more take must FAIL
  // rather than re-serve a live slot.
  let oc = 0
  while (oc < OCC_SPANS) {                              // bounded: OCC_SPANS
    if (allocSpanFree(occSpan[oc]) != 0) { occOk = 0 }
    oc = oc + 1
  }
  spinAcquire(lk3)
  let occExtra = heapTakeSmallLocked(h3, OCC_CLASS, occCs)
  spinRelease(lk3)
  if (occExtra != 0) { occOk = 0 }

  // A deterministic Fisher-Yates over the slot indices. A permutation by
  // construction, so no address can be freed twice if `perSpan` ever moves.
  let occOrder = []i64(occN)
  let po = 0
  while (po < occN) { occOrder[po] = po; po = po + 1 }  // bounded: occN
  let seed = 12345
  let sw = occN - 1
  while (sw > 0) {                                      // bounded: occN - 1
    seed = (seed * 1103515245 + 12345) % 2147483648
    let pick = seed % (sw + 1)
    let tmp = occOrder[sw]
    occOrder[sw] = occOrder[pick]
    occOrder[pick] = tmp
    sw = sw - 1
  }

  // The property: after EVERY free, each span's own free count equals the tally
  // kept here from the addresses alone.
  let fi = 0
  while (fi < occN) {                                   // bounded: occN
    let k = occOrder[fi]
    spinAcquire(lk3)
    heapGiveSmallLocked(h3, occAddr[k], OCC_CLASS, occCs)
    spinRelease(lk3)
    let own = occOwner[k]
    occFreed[own] = occFreed[own] + 1
    let cj = 0
    while (cj < OCC_SPANS) {                            // bounded: OCC_SPANS
      if (allocSpanFree(occSpan[cj]) != occFreed[cj]) { occOk = 0 }
      cj = cj + 1
    }
    fi = fi + 1
  }

  // Everything back: each span wholly free, no live bytes, and the runtime's own
  // cross-check of `sum over spans of (total - free)` against the class's live
  // slot count agrees.
  let oe = 0
  while (oe < OCC_SPANS) {                              // bounded: OCC_SPANS
    if (occFreed[oe] != perSpan) { occOk = 0 }
    if (allocSpanFree(occSpan[oe]) != perSpan) { occOk = 0 }
    oe = oe + 1
  }
  if (heapLiveBytes(h3) != 0) { occOk = 0 }
  spinAcquire(lk3)
  let occBad = heapCheckSpansLocked(h3)
  spinRelease(lk3)
  if (occBad != 0) { occOk = 0 }
  if (heapSpanViolations(h3) != 0) { occOk = 0 }

  // ---- (i) the direct-mapped (large) path ----------------------------------
  // Requests above allocMaxSmall bypass the size classes entirely: they are
  // mapped straight from the OS, rounded up to a page, and accounted through
  // `heapLargeAllocLocked`/`heapLargeFreeLocked`. Nothing above reaches that
  // path — every size so far is <= allocMaxSmall and so classifies as SMALL —
  // and the Zig has a dedicated unit test for it ("large direct-mapped
  // allocations are page aligned and unmapped on free"), so it is mirrored
  // here rather than left to the differential, which only covers the
  // `allocLargeLen` arithmetic and not its composition with the counters.
  let largeOk = 1
  let liveBefore = heapLiveBytes(h)
  let mappedBefore = heapMappedBytes(h)
  let li = 0
  while (li < 3) {
    let lsz = allocMaxSmall + 1
    if (li == 1) { lsz = 100000 }
    if (li == 2) { lsz = 1048576 }
    if (allocClassify(lsz, 16) >= 0) { largeOk = 0 }   // must NOT be a size class
    let want = allocLargeLen(lsz, PAGE)
    let addr = heapAlloc(h, lk, a, lsz, 16)
    if (addr == 0) {
      largeOk = 0
    } else {
      if (addr % PAGE != 0) { largeOk = 0 }            // direct maps are page aligned
      if (heapLiveBytes(h) != liveBefore + lsz) { largeOk = 0 }
      if (heapMappedBytes(h) != mappedBefore + want) { largeOk = 0 }
      // The whole rounded-up length is real, writable memory.
      let w = wordAt(addr)
      *w = 424242
      let wEnd = wordAt(addr + want - 8)
      *wEnd = 515151
      let back = *w
      if (back != 424242) { largeOk = 0 }
      heapFree(h, lk, addr, lsz, 16)
      if (heapLiveBytes(h) != liveBefore) { largeOk = 0 }
      if (heapMappedBytes(h) != mappedBefore) { largeOk = 0 }   // mapping returned
    }
    li = li + 1
  }

  // ---- (g)+(h) churn with sentinels ----------------------------------------
  let ringAddr = []i64(RING)
  let ringSize = []i64(RING)
  let ringTag = []i64(RING)
  let intact = 0
  let checked = 0
  let churnOk = 1
  let n = 0
  while (n < CHURN) {
    let slot = n % RING
    let old = ringAddr[slot]
    if (old != 0) {
      let w = wordAt(old)
      let seen = *w
      checked = checked + 1
      if (seen == ringTag[slot]) { intact = intact + 1 }
      heapFree(h, lk, old, ringSize[slot], 8)
      ringAddr[slot] = 0
    }
    // Deterministic sizes spanning many classes, staying inside the arena.
    let sz = 1 + (n * 37) % 3000
    let addr = heapAlloc(h, lk, a, sz, 8)
    if (addr == 0) {
      churnOk = 0                                 // arena exhausted: never deref
    } else {
      if (addr % 8 != 0) { churnOk = 0 }
      let tag = n * 2654435761 + 1
      let w2 = wordAt(addr)
      *w2 = tag                                   // sentinel in the first word
      ringAddr[slot] = addr
      ringSize[slot] = sz
      ringTag[slot] = tag
    }
    n = n + 1
  }
  // Drain survivors.
  let d = 0
  while (d < RING) {
    let old = ringAddr[d]
    if (old != 0) {
      let w = wordAt(old)
      let seen = *w
      checked = checked + 1
      if (seen == ringTag[d]) { intact = intact + 1 }
      heapFree(h, lk, old, ringSize[d], 8)
      ringAddr[d] = 0
    }
    d = d + 1
  }
  let liveAtEnd = heapLiveBytes(h)
  let mappedAtEnd = heapMappedBytes(h)
  let spansUsed = a[A_SPANS]

  // The same cross-check over the heap every other property ran against, after
  // 20000 rounds of churn across 37 classes rather than one class in isolation.
  spinAcquire(lk)
  let mainBad = heapCheckSpansLocked(h)
  spinRelease(lk)

  // `arena` is reached here so it stays rooted across every collection above.
  let arenaRooted = 0
  if (len(arena) == ARENA_WORDS) { arenaRooted = 1 }

  print("tbl=${tsum}/${twsum} mono=${mono} pows=${powsOk} cls=${classifyOk} init=${initOk} align=${alignOk}/${liveAfterAlign} reuse=${reuseOk} carve=${carveOk}/${got} occ=${occOk}/${occN} large=${largeOk} churn=${churnOk} sent=${intact}/${checked} live=${liveAtEnd} mapped=${mappedAtEnd}/${spansUsed} chk=${mainBad} root=${arenaRooted}\n")
}
